Yes. Under **Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2 (CAP/DA), Section 3.3 “Transfers of Eligible Beneficiaries”**, a CAP/DA beneficiary **can transfer between service providers/case management entities**, and the policy says the **CME shall coordinate the transfer to prevent gaps in service provisions**. The policy does **not** use the phrase “service provider” in a general sense here; it focuses on **transfer coordination between provider agencies / CME designees**.  
**Citation:** Section **sec:doc:clinical_policy:3K-2:3.3** (Policy **3K-2**). I checked amendments to this document, and **no amendments were listed**.

## Rules governing transfers of eligible beneficiaries
Section 3.3 says these steps must be completed **prior to the transfer**:

- determine anticipated start date of service;
- coordinate transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the health, safety and well-being of the beneficiary;
- initiate in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) the transfer of the electronic health records to the receiving county;
- arrange for a home visit within **five (5) business days** of the date the CAP/DA beneficiary moves to their new primary residence by the receiving entity to assess the home environment and identify any health and welfare concerns and plan for mitigation and safety; and
- coordinate and plan the provision of services to start on the **first date of the transfer**.

### Special transfer situations
The same section also gives rules for:

- **CAP/C to CAP/DA aging-out transfers**:
  - transition plan begins **12 months prior to the birth month**;
  - a multidisciplinary team meeting must be held **three months (90 calendar days) prior** to the birth month or identified transfer month;
  - the local DSS must be notified **the month prior** to change the CAP evidence code;
  - CAP/DA services are authorized and provided on the **first day of the birth month or identified transfer month**.

- **CAP/DA transfers to a different county**:
  - the transferring CME designee must coordinate with the receiving CME designee **on the agreed upon transfer date or less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer**;
  - the electronic health record is transferred to the receiving county;
  - the receiving CME designee must arrange the home visit within **five (5) business days** after the beneficiary moves; and
  - services should start on the **first date of transfer into the receiving county**.

